\description{
Simulates a sample size plan for the coefficient of variation to show
what it delivers when the population value differs from the planning
value, or at a sample size chosen in advance. It takes the population
coefficient of variation, either a planning value or a sample size, and
a target width, and returns summaries of the estimate, the interval
width, the proportion of intervals no wider than the target, and
coverage over \code{G} simulated normal samples (Kelley, 2007; Maxwell,
Delaney, & Kelley, 2027, Section 3.11 on power-based sample size
planning).
}
\details{
Sample size planning for the coefficient of variation under the Accuracy in Parameter Estimation
framework chooses \emph{N} so that the expected (or, with assurance, the high-probability) confidence
interval width is no larger than \code{width} (Kelley, 2007). Because the procedure assumes the
planning value \code{supposed_cv} matches the population value \code{true_cv}, in practice
the realized width will deviate from the planned width whenever the planning value is wrong. This
sensitivity analysis quantifies the deviation by Monte Carlo simulation: the planned \emph{N} is
obtained from \code{\link{ss_aipe_cv}} with \code{supposed_cv}, then samples are drawn from the
\emph{true} population (with coefficient of variation \code{true_cv}) and the realized confidence
interval widths are summarized.

For planning a sample size so that a confidence interval is sufficiently narrow, see Maxwell, Delaney,
& Kelley (2027, Section 3.9.1.5, and Section 4.9 on assurance).

The samples are drawn from a normal population, which the interval and the plan assume, so the
function does not show what a plan delivers for a population that is not normal. With a skewed
population the coverage of the interval departs from the stated level: in 40,000 samples at each of
the three plans for a coefficient of variation of .50, a width of .20, and 95 percent confidence (79
without \code{assurance}, 97 with an assurance of .80, and 142 with .99), coverage was .97 for a gamma
population with that coefficient of variation, and for a lognormal one it was .915, .913, and .905,
lowest at the largest plan. The proportion of intervals no wider than \code{width} was .55, .88, and
1.00 for the gamma population and .59, .84, and .99 for the lognormal one. For a normal population
that probability, computed from the noncentral \emph{t} distribution, is .53, .84, and 1.00 (see
\code{\link{ss_aipe_cv}} and \code{\link{ci_cv}}).
}
\section{The record of the simulation}{

Every sensitivity function of the package summarizes its replications
over one denominator: the replications that produced a confidence
interval (for a power sensitivity function, a test). The proportion of
intervals that met the width target (\code{pct_ci_less_width}), the
proportions of intervals that missed the true value, and the summaries
of the widths are all taken over those replications. A replication
without an interval is in none of these rows. It is counted, and the
returned table carries the counts in its attribute \code{simulation},
a list read with \code{attr(x, "simulation")}:
\describe{
  \item{\code{attempted}}{the number of replications asked for
    (\code{G}).}
  \item{\code{returned}}{the number that produced an interval (a
    test).}
  \item{\code{failed}}{the number that did not
    (\code{attempted - returned}).}
  \item{\code{denominator}}{the number of replications the rows named
    above are taken over, which is \code{returned}.}
  \item{\code{width_target_probability}}{the proportion of the
    replications with an interval whose interval met the width target,
    the value of the \code{pct_ci_less_width} row; \code{NA} for a
    function or a call without a width target, and when no replication
    produced an interval.}
  \item{\code{width_target_mc_ci}}{the exact binomial
    (Clopper-Pearson) limits for that proportion, at the confidence
    level of the table. They describe the error of the simulation, which
    shrinks as \code{G} grows, and nothing else.}
}
A function that records more about its run adds elements to the list,
and its help page names them. The line "Replications" beneath the
printed table gives \code{G}, and the three counts when a replication
was without an interval. With many replications without an interval the
rows describe only the studies in which the interval could be formed,
so \code{failed} is to be reported beside them.
}

\examples{
# Textbook scenario (Kelley, 2007). A researcher plans a study to estimate the
# coefficient of variation of reaction times with a 95\% confidence interval no
# wider than .10. They guess from prior work that the population coefficient of
# variation is around .25 and apply ss_aipe_cv() to obtain a planned N.

# Question 1: how does the realized interval width behave when the planning
# value is correct (well-specified case)? G = 100 keeps the example quick;
# a reported sensitivity analysis deserves the default G = 1000 or more.
set.seed(113)
ss_aipe_cv_sensitivity(
  true_cv          = .25,
  supposed_cv      = .25,
  width            = .10,
  assurance        = NULL,
  conf_level       = .95,
  G                = 100,
  print_iter       = FALSE
)

# Question 2: what happens if the planning value is materially smaller than
# the true coefficient of variation (a common direction of misspecification,
# since planning values are often optimistic)? The intervals will be wider on
# average than the target and the pct_ci_less_width will fall.
set.seed(113)
ss_aipe_cv_sensitivity(
  true_cv          = .35,
  supposed_cv      = .25,
  width            = .10,
  assurance        = NULL,
  conf_level       = .95,
  G                = 100,
  print_iter       = FALSE
)

}
